WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:00.810
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
There wouldn't be

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:02.550
much point to a
job search solution

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.410
without jobs to search for.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.630
In our last video, we talked
about creating companies

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.840
using the Google Cloud Talent
Solution Job Search API.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.413
Now, let's talk about how to
create jobs and best practices

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.330
for working with them.

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.655
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.870
We'll get started
by creating a job.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:22.610
You can't make a job
without a company,

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.630
so we'll be using a company that
we created in the last video.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:27.980
There are also a few
required fields we'll need.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.840
First off, we'll need
the company's name,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.690
which is the unique
identifier, not to be

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:33.430
confused with display name.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.115
We'll also need
a requisition ID,

00:00:35.115 --> 00:00:36.490
which is a string
that represents

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.890
your own unique identifier
for the job, kind

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.793
of like external
ID for companies.

00:00:40.793 --> 00:00:42.460
This is helpful to
make sure it's easier

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.120
to sync the job search API
in your current job solution.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.950
There's also the
title, which is, well,

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.630
the display title of the job.

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:52.270
Description is where you put
all the job details and related

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.260
information.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.900
You can use HTML and
some markup tags here.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.120
Finally, we'll need at
least one of three fields

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.800
under application info--

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.425
URIs, emails, or instruction.

00:01:02.425 --> 00:01:04.300
These help point job
seekers to where they'll

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.400
need to go in order to apply.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.620
URIs and emails
are arrays in case

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:10.540
you have multiple,
while instruction

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.150
is an HTML-friendly string
where you can describe

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:14.980
the application process.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.100
Each job could have all three,
but you can't create a job

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.660
without having at least one.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:21.550
With all those fields,
you can create a job,

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:22.858
so let's look at some code.

00:01:22.858 --> 00:01:24.400
The code structure
should look pretty

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.040
similar to our other samples,
but let's walk through the bits

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.540
to create the job.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.910
We need to include the project
ID and the company name

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.790
here so we can use it when
we first define the job.

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.190
We'll hardcode it
just for this snippet.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:37.810
Here, we're passing
in the job when we

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.170
call the API with jobs.create.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.040
Again, pretty similar
to creating a company.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.950
And here's where we
actually define the job

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:46.720
and set those required fields.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.150
We've got the company name,
which was set up above,

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.950
as well as the
requisition ID, which

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.070
is the string we can use to more
easily map the job to our job

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:54.940
system.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.680
After that, there's the title,
description, and in this case,

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.560
we provided a link for the
application information.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.140
So from here, we'll
create the job.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.580
And the API knows which
company it belongs to,

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.550
so we pass in the company name.

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:09.350
After running that code,
here's part of the response.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.000
We'll see the new
job was created,

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.420
and it has the same property
values that we passed in,

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.160
as well as our
unique name that we

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.960
can use when referencing a job.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.450
Now that we can
create jobs, what

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.200
happens when we try
to create a job that's

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.000
already in the system?

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.830
As you might expect,
you'll get an error,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.110
because job duplicates
aren't allowed.

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:28.570
Duplicates are determined
by three fields--

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:31.930
company name, requisition
ID, and language code.

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.240
We provided company
name and requisition ID

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.860
when creating the job.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.750
Language code is
an optional field

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.660
that will represent the language
based on the job description,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.960
and it defaults to
en us if not set.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.990
As long as the combination of
these three fields is unique,

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.490
the job won't be
treated as a duplicate.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.850
So that's the basics
for creating jobs.

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:54.190
We can also get a job
to see the details,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:57.040
delete a job to get rid
of it, and patch a job

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.990
to update any properties.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.990
Just like companies, you should
pass in an update mask, which

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:04.240
is a string of
comma-separated properties

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:05.470
that you want to update.

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:06.970
There's a few more
details about why

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:09.550
that's important in
the company's video.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:12.830
We can also list jobs to see
all the jobs in the system.

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.070
This is just a simple list
without any filtering,

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:17.320
so it's probably better
saved for any administrative

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:19.030
functionality.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.520
This code is the modified
version of our list companies

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.310
code that now shows
companies and jobs.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:26.830
We'll loop through each
company, and then list jobs

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:29.500
for that company by filtering
on the company's name.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.450
Pretty simple, but a
great starting point

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:33.280
to making sure that
you know the jobs

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.798
and companies in your system.

00:03:34.798 --> 00:03:37.090
There's quite a few more
fields when dealing with jobs,

00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:38.368
but they're optional.

00:03:38.368 --> 00:03:40.160
Let's look at some of
the more common ones.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.760
And you can always check on
the docs to see the full list.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.610
Each job can have an array
of up to 50 addresses, which

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:46.990
are passed in as strings.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:48.610
Just like companies,
the API will

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.240
create a read-only property for
each job, called derived info,

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.190
that looks at
addresses you pass in

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:56.170
as well as the parent
company's information

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:57.970
to geolocate each job.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.860
This is all powered by the
Google Maps Geocoding API.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:03.190
So if it works there,
it'll work here.

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:04.840
There's also a
posting region which

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.420
lets you specify how strict the
location matching for the job

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:08.600
needs to be.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:11.380
For instance, if the posting
region is set to nation,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.300
this job will show up
for a search in jobs

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.920
that match the same country.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.320
Administrative area
depends on the country

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.870
but represents areas like
states, for United States,

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.000
and prefectures for Japan.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.550
Telecommute means the job
allows for remote work,

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.507
and the location
is less relevant.

00:04:26.507 --> 00:04:28.090
There are also some
fields to help you

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.400
talk more about the job
itself, such as job benefits,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.095
compensation info, employment
types, and degree types.

00:04:35.095 --> 00:04:36.970
Each of these have their
own object, or enum,

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.680
to get the most accurate data.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.990
But they're all
pretty simple fields.

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:42.530
There's even custom attributes.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.358
So you can provide your
own key value pairs

00:04:44.358 --> 00:04:46.150
and do some advanced
searching, which we'll

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:47.920
talk about in a later video.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.440
However, these are all useful
to help jobs searchers get

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.550
better results.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:54.130
There are also a few properties
specific to the job posting

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:55.190
itself.

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.340
These are all strings
that represent timestamps.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.315
The fields postingCreateTime
and postingUpdateTime

00:05:00.315 --> 00:05:01.690
are read-only
fields that let you

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.150
know when the job posting
was created and most recently

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.040
updated, respectively.

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.040
jobStartTime and jobEndTime
are useful for contracting

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.350
or seasonal jobs where you
might have specific timing

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.670
requirements.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.320
postingPublishTime
represents when

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.540
this job was most
recently published,

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:18.780
while postingExpireTime
is the timestamp for when

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:20.730
the job posting expires.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.460
By default, the expiration
time is set to 30 days

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:25.110
after the job was created.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.120
We definitely recommend
explicitly setting

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.010
the expiration date
to what makes the most

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:30.490
sense for your business.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.760
For example, if you update
a job but don't include

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.730
a new expiration time, it
will refresh the expiration

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.010
to 30 days from the
time of the update.

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:39.930
If you want to get
around that, explicitly

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.480
set a new expiration
time, or make

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.140
sure to use an update mask
without posting expire time.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.300
Expired jobs won't show
up in search results

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.670
or when calling list,
but you can still

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:54.200
get the properties of an expired
job by using get, and in fact,

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.740
update it with patch.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.320
It's even possible to update
the posting expire time

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.570
to unexpire a job.

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.007
That patch may fail if
there's another open job

00:06:03.007 --> 00:06:05.340
with the same company name,
requisition ID, and language

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.200
code, so make sure
that you handle

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.210
any errors that come back.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.340
Also, if a job is
expired for long enough,

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:14.700
or you have too many expired
jobs compared to open jobs,

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:17.460
expired jobs start to
get permanently deleted.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.160
Remember, the job search API
is a search index for jobs.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.470
So if you want any
permanent record keeping,

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.682
you should do that in the
job solution directly.

00:06:25.682 --> 00:06:28.140
That's a brief overview to help
you start loading companies

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:28.660
and jobs.

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.410
With all that data,
there's only one thing

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.890
to talk about in
our next video--

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:33.307
performing searches.

00:06:33.307 --> 00:06:34.140
Thanks for watching.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.940
And remember, when
looking for talent,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:37.565
it's OK to keep your
head in the cloud.

00:06:37.565 --> 00:06:40.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

